titre $Crepidotus,  KEY TO THE EUROPEAN SPECIES 
com$  Senn-Irle, Persoonia (Note: excellent optical equipment is crucial for identification!) / adapt GF 08
1,2 $ a. All hyphae of fruit body without clamp connections
1,6 $ b. All hyphae of fruit body with clamp connections
2,3 $ a. Spores ornamented (sometimes very faintly; oil immersion!)
2,4 $ b. Spores absolutely smooth, never ornamented
3,0 $ a. Spores globose to broadly ellipsoid (Q = 1.0 1.3), minutely warty, punctate under the light microscope, 6 8 x 5.5 6.5 µm, with moderately coloured walls; fruit body red $C. cinnabarinus  8. 
3,0 $ b. Spores ellipsoid to oblong subcylindrical (Q = 1.6 2. 1), minutely rugulose to al most smooth, 8.5 12 x 5 6.5 µm, with faintly coloured walls; fruit body white $C. versutus  17. 
4,5 $ a. Spores broadly ellipsoid to ellipsoid (Q < 1.7), 7 10 x 5 7 µm, in side view often amygdaliform, with strongly coloured walls; trama of pileus with gelatinous layer; cheilocystidia narrowly utriform to cylindrical 
4,0 $ b. Spores cylindrical, fusoid to pip shaped (Q = 2.1 3.2), 6.5 9.5 x 2.5 3.5 µm, with weakly coloured walls; trama of pileus without gelatinous layer; cheilocystidia whip like, filiform $C. epibryus 16
5,0 $ a. Fruit body with macroscopically recognizable ferruginous scales; spores 7 10 x 5 7 µm (Q = 1. 3  1.7) $C. calolepis  2. 
5,0 $ b. Fruit-body without macroscopically recognizable scales, cream, yellowish, pale brown; spores 7 10 x 5 6.5 µm (Q = 1.3 1.7) $C. mollis 1. 
6,7 $ a. Spores globose to broadly ellipsoid (mean Q < 1.35), echinulate, spiny or punetate under the light microscope
6,13 $ b. Spores ellipsoid, in side view sometimes amygdaliform (mean Q > 1.4), never echinulate but verruculose or rugulose, warty or smooth under the light microscope
7,8 $ a. Cheilocystidia with short finger like protuberances, small, < 30 µm long
7,9 $ b. Cheilocystidia without finger like protuberances, > 30 µm long
8,0 $ a. Spores 6 8 x 5  7 µm (Q = 1.2 1.5), fruit-body pinkish red $C. roseoornatus 7. 
8,0 $ b. Spores 5 6.5 x 4.5 6 µm (Q = 1 1.2), fruit body white or yellow when young $C. carpaticus 6. 
9,11 $ a. Spores 4.5 7.5 x 5 7 µm, distinctly punctate warty, baculate, moderately thick- walled; cheilocystidia never exclusively antler like, mostly clavate or cylindrical, flexuous and often branched; lamellae never pink, crowded
9,10 $ b. Spores 6 9 x 4.5 7.5 µm, minutely spiny, echinulate, orpunctate warty, rather thin walled; cheilocystidia narrowly lageniform, rarely clavate, branched and often antlerlike; lamellae sometimes pinkish, fairly crowded to distant
10,0 $ a. Spores 6 9 x 5.5 7.5 µm (Q = 1.1 1.35), echinulate; fruit body < 30 mm, thin; young lamellae often pinkish, subdistant to distant $C. cesatii 10. 
10,0 $ b. Spores 6 8 x 4.5 6 µm (Q = 1.25 1.5), punctate warty; fruit-body > 30 mm, fleshy; lamellae crowded, never pinkish $C. macedonicus 9. 
11,0 $ a. Pileus densely covered with orange fulvous scales, never distinctly hygrophanous $C. crocophyllus 4. 
11,12 $ b. Pileus without scales, often distinctly hygrophanous
12,0 $ a. Cheilocystidia cylindrical, flexuous, often branched; pileipellis a true trichoderm; pileus rounded flabelliform, semicircular, light orange to melon yellow; young lamelIae pale orange $C. ehrendorferi 5. 
12,0 $ b. Cheilocystidia clavate or lageniform, never flexuous, very rarely mixed with some apically branched ones; pileipellis a cutis with scattered clusters of pileocystidia; pileus often spathuliform, cream to greyish brown; young lamellae cream to clay $C. applanatus 3. 
13,14 $ a. Spores faintly rugulose or completely smooth; lamellae never pink
13,15 $ b. Spores distinctly rugulose or verruculose; lamellae occasionally pinkish or yellow when young
14,0 $ a. Spores completely smooth, 7 9 x 5 6 µm (Q = 13 1.7), with strongly coloured, thick walls; fruit body often fan shaped, terrestrial $C. autochthonus 15. 
14,0 $ b. Spores faintly rugulose, 6.5 9.5 x 4.3 6 µm (Q = 1.4 1.7), with moderately to faintly coloured walls; fruit body semicircular, lignicolous $C. lundellii .12. 
15,0 $ a. Spores ellipsoid to amygdaliform, rugulose, 7.3 10.5 x 5 6.5 µm (Q = 1.4 1.7); cheilocystidia narrowly lageniform $C. subverrucisporus 14. 
15,16 $ b. Spores elongate to cylindrical (mean Q > 1.7); cheilocystidia cylindrical, flexuous or clavate, obpyriform, sometimes branched
16,0 $ a. Spores subcylindrical, fusiform, 8 10 x 4 5 µm (Q = 1.8 2.1); cheilocystidia narrowly cylindrical, flexuous, sometimes branched; fruit body either fresh or dried lemon yellow, rarely cream white, margin rarely lobed $C. luteolus 13. 
16,0 $ b. Spores cylindrical, 6 9 x 3 4.5 µm (Q = 1.6 2.2); fruit body white, margin often lobed to crenate $C. variabilis 11. 
